        <div id="efflorescence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Efflorescence, Spalling and Other Problems Sealer Will and Will Not Fix
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Efflorescence</strong>, the white powdery deposit on brick, comes from soluble salts carried to the surface by water as it evaporates. Sealing can reduce future efflorescence by cutting how much water enters the wall, but it does not remove existing deposits, and sealing over active efflorescence can push salt crystallization just under the surface, where it does more damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean efflorescence first. Dry brushing removes much of it. Stubborn deposits can be cleaned with a masonry cleaner made for efflorescence, following the label, then rinsed thoroughly. Wait until the wall stays clean through a few dry weeks, which shows the moisture source is under control, before sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Spalling</strong> brick should be replaced, not sealed. Once the face has broken, the softer inner body of the brick is exposed and absorbs even more water. Replacing individual units and fixing the water source is the repair. Sealer can then protect the rest of the wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Moss and algae</strong> grow on brick that stays damp, usually on north-facing or shaded walls. Clean them with a biocidal masonry cleaner and a soft brush rather than high-pressure washing. A water repellent applied afterward helps the wall dry faster, which makes regrowth slower.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Choose the Right Brick Sealer
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Exterior walls of a house</strong>: choose a breathable silane-siloxane repellent such as Ghostshield 8500, Masonry Defender or Foundation Armor SX5000 WB. Look for a product rated for vertical above-grade masonry and confirm the data sheet describes it as vapor permeable.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Chimneys</strong>: the same type, with extra emphasis on breathability, because chimneys take rain from every side and also release moisture from inside. DRYLOK Natural Look and the Ghostshield products both suit chimney brick. Our dedicated <a href="/best-chimney-sealer/">chimney sealer guide</a> covers crowns, flashing and the parts of a chimney a repellent cannot fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Brick patios and walkways</strong>: use a penetrating repellent rated for horizontal surfaces, or an enhancing version if you want richer color. Avoid high-gloss films outdoors, which can be slippery when wet. For mortarless brick pavers set in sand, our <a href="/best-paver-sealer/">paver sealer guide</a> addresses joint stabilization as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Interior brick and fireplace surrounds</strong>: here a film-forming sealer such as Seal-Krete Clear-Seal is acceptable, and its sheen and dust control are often the point. Keep it off the firebox itself, and use only products whose label approves them for fireplace surrounds. For concrete block foundations, see our <a href="/best-cinder-block-sealer-reviews/">cinder block sealer guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Preparing Brick for Sealing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean the brick so the sealer penetrates evenly. Use a masonry cleaner suited to the soil: a biocidal cleaner for algae, an efflorescence cleaner for white salt deposits, or a general-purpose masonry detergent for dirt. Scrub with a stiff natural or nylon brush, never a wire brush on soft brick, which leaves marks and can embed metal that rusts.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you use a pressure washer, keep the pressure modest and the nozzle well back from the surface. High pressure erodes mortar joints and the fired skin of soft brick, which leaves the wall more absorbent than before. A wide fan tip and a distance of a foot or more is a sensible starting point; our <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">pressure washer guide</a> covers safe settings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make repairs before sealing. Repoint eroded joints, replace spalled brick, and caulk gaps around windows, doors and penetrations. Sealer is not a filler. Fresh mortar needs time to cure before sealing; many repointing mortar makers recommend weeks, not days, so check the product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, let the wall dry. Most silane-siloxane labels call for a surface dry to the touch with at least 24 hours since rain or washing, and temperatures in a mild band, often around 40 to 90 degrees Fahrenheit. Protect plants, windows and trim from overspray with plastic sheeting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Applying Sealer to Brick
          </h2>

          <p>
            Low-pressure pump sprayers are the standard tool for penetrating repellents on walls. Apply from the bottom up, flooding the surface until the sealer runs down six to eight inches below the spray pattern. That rundown is how you know the brick is saturated. Back-brush or back-roll any puddles on horizontal ledges.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many silane-siloxane products specify a second coat applied wet on wet, meaning before the first has dried. Read the label: the timing matters, because once the first coat reacts, the surface repels the second. On very porous brick, a second coat is what achieves the coverage depth that gives the product its stated life.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work in sections that can be completed without lap marks, typically from one natural break, such as a corner or a downspout, to the next. Clean overspray from glass immediately with the solvent the label lists; silane-siloxane residue on windows is hard to remove once it reacts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan around weather. Avoid application in direct hot sun, which can flash the carrier off before the active ingredients penetrate, and avoid days with rain expected within the label's window, often 12 to 24 hours. The water repellency typically develops over a few days, so do not judge results by spraying the wall the next morning.
          </p>

