        <div id="coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Coverage and Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coverage for brick sealers depends almost entirely on porosity. A dense, hard-fired face brick may take a gallon over 150 to 250 square feet, while soft or sand-molded brick may take a gallon per 75 to 125 square feet. Labels quote a range; start by sealing a measured section and calculating how much you actually used before buying for the whole house.
          </p>

          <p>
            To estimate wall area, multiply wall length by height for each face and subtract large openings like garage doors. A typical two-story brick house can have 2,000 square feet or more of brick, which is 10 to 20 gallons or more for one coat on porous brick. A chimney might be 100 to 200 square feet; a patio is just its floor area.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prices at the time of writing run roughly $25 to $50 per gallon for big-box multi-surface products, $50 to $100 for mid-range masonry repellents, and $90 to $150 for premium concentrated silane-siloxanes. Premium products often cover more area per gallon, which narrows the gap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional brick sealing typically costs $1 to $3 per square foot, more if it includes cleaning, repointing or work at height. For a single-story wall reachable from a ladder, DIY is very manageable. Two-story walls, chimneys and steep roofs are where a contractor with staging and fall protection is worth paying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Long Brick Sealer Lasts and When to Reapply
          </h2>

          <p>
            Penetrating silane-siloxane repellents commonly carry life claims of 5 to 10 years on vertical surfaces, and some premium products claim more. Horizontal surfaces such as patios and wall caps wear faster from foot traffic, standing water and sun, often needing attention every 3 to 5 years.
          </p>

          <p>
            The hose test from earlier is also the maintenance test. When water stops beading and the brick darkens quickly and stays dark, the repellent is fading. There is no need to strip penetrating sealers; clean the wall and apply another coat the same way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Film-forming sealers behave differently. Once they wear, they look patchy and may peel. Recoating over a degraded film gives uneven results; the old film often has to be stripped with a chemical stripper first, which is a messy job on textured brick.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between treatments, keep gutters clean, maintain caulk at windows and penetrations, and repair mortar as soon as joints start to erode. These details keep more water off and out of the wall than any sealer, and they make each coat of repellent last longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="patios" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Brick Patios, Walkways and Steps
          </h2>

          <p>
            Horizontal brick has a harder life than a wall. Rain sits on it instead of running off, foot traffic wears the surface, leaves and planters leave tannin and rust stains, and in cold regions it may get de-icing salt. Brick pavers set in sand also have joints that wash out and sprout weeds, which a sealer only partly addresses.
          </p>

          <p>
            For mortared brick patios and steps, a penetrating repellent rated for horizontal surfaces is the safest choice. It keeps the brick from soaking up water and reduces freeze damage, and it does not change traction. If the brick has faded, an enhancing penetrating product such as Ghostshield 8510 restores color without the slick surface of a gloss film.
          </p>

          <p>
            For brick pavers on a sand bed, you have two goals: protect the brick and lock the joints. Some paver sealers are designed to harden joint sand as well as repel water. Refill the joints first, preferably with polymeric sand where appropriate, then seal according to the product directions once everything is dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid high-gloss acrylic films on walkways and pool decks. They look striking on a dry day but can become slippery when wet, and they wear unevenly along traffic paths, leaving patchy dull lanes. If you want a wet look on a patio, choose a breathable enhancing repellent and accept a more subtle sheen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="retaining-walls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Retaining Walls, Garden Walls and Foundations
          </h2>

          <p>
            Retaining walls are among the brick structures most likely to benefit from sealing, and among the most likely to be damaged by the wrong product. Soil behind the wall keeps the back of the brick damp, so water is always moving through toward the exposed face. A film on that face traps the water and almost guarantees spalling and efflorescence in cold climates.
          </p>

          <p>
            The right approach is drainage first: a gravel backfill zone, a perforated drain at the base, and weep holes that let water escape. Then, if the exposed face still absorbs heavily, treat it with a breathable penetrating repellent. Do not seal the back face or try to waterproof it from the front with coatings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Freestanding garden walls get wet from the top. A sound coping or cap, with a drip edge that throws water clear of the face, does most of the work. Seal the cap and the top courses generously, since they take the most water, and check cap joints regularly, as cracks there send water straight down into the core.
          </p>

          <p>
            Below-grade brick foundations and basement walls face different problems, mainly hydrostatic pressure and wicking from soil. Clear above-grade repellents are not designed for that. For walls that leak into a basement, see our <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/">basement wall sealer guide</a>, which covers products meant for below-grade masonry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="interior-brick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Interior Brick Walls and Exposed Brick
          </h2>

          <p>
            Exposed brick inside a home rarely needs water protection. The reasons to seal it are cosmetic and practical: old brick can shed sand and dust, absorb cooking grease in a kitchen, and look dry or chalky. A clear sealer binds loose particles, makes the wall easier to wipe, and can deepen the color.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here, film-forming products like Seal-Krete Clear-Seal are a reasonable choice because the wall is not exposed to rain or freeze-thaw. Choose matte or satin if you want the brick to look natural; gloss can look plastic on rough brick. Apply thin coats with a roller or brush, working the product into the mortar joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before sealing, vacuum the wall with a brush attachment, then wipe it with a damp cloth to pick up remaining dust. Let it dry fully. If the brick was recently exposed by removing plaster, check for residual plaster or adhesive that can show through as blotches once sealed, and clean those off first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test the product on a few bricks in a hidden corner. Some old brick darkens dramatically under any sealer, and a test is the only way to know whether you like the result. Ventilate well while the sealer cures, even with water-based products.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a glossy film on exterior brick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acrylic and polyurethane films look appealing at first but trap moisture in exterior brick, especially in freeze-thaw climates. The result can be peeling film, cloudy patches and spalled brick faces within a few winters. Use a breathable penetrating repellent outdoors and keep film-formers for interior or covered surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over efflorescence</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                White salt deposits show the wall is moving water. Sealing over them can trap salts just under the surface, where crystallization breaks off the brick face. Clean the deposits, fix the moisture source, and confirm the wall stays clean through a dry spell before applying any sealer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping mortar repairs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer cannot fill eroded or cracked joints, and most water enters walls through mortar rather than brick. Repoint failing joints first and give the new mortar the cure time its maker specifies. Sealing a wall with open joints wastes product and leaves the leak paths unchanged.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying too little product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Penetrating repellents work by depth. A light misting looks finished but leaves only a shallow treated layer that wears off quickly. Flood the surface until it runs down several inches, and apply a second coat wet on wet if the label calls for it. Thirsty brick needs more than the label minimum.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blasting brick with high pressure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Aggressive pressure washing erodes mortar and strips the fired skin from softer brick, leaving a rougher, more absorbent surface than before you started. Use a masonry cleaner, a stiff brush and low pressure from a safe distance, and let the wall dry fully before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a product sold for concrete work on a brick wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes. Many penetrating silane-siloxane sealers are labeled for concrete, brick, block and pavers, and those work on brick. Film-forming concrete sealers, such as high-gloss acrylics meant for driveways, are a poor choice for exterior brick walls because they limit breathability. Check that the label lists brick and describes the product as vapor permeable before using it on a wall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a realistic reapplication schedule for brick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With a quality penetrating repellent, walls often go 5 to 10 years between treatments, according to typical manufacturer claims, while patios and caps need attention sooner. Use the hose test every year or two: if water soaks in and the brick stays dark, it is time. Film-forming sealers need recoating more often, sometimes every one to three years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a clear repellent darken or shine brick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard penetrating repellents are designed to leave brick looking the same once they dry, though it may look darker for a few hours while wet. Enhancing products like Ghostshield 8510 intentionally darken it, and acrylic films add a sheen and deepen color. Test any product on a hidden area and view it fully dry before sealing a whole wall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a repellent cure white salt deposits on a wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can reduce future efflorescence by limiting how much water enters the brick, but it does not remove existing deposits and will not stop efflorescence driven by water from behind or below the wall. Clean the deposits, fix gutters, grading and flashing, and seal only once the wall stays dry. Sealing over active salts can damage the brick.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to treat brick around a fireplace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can seal the brick surround and hearth with a product rated for fireplace use, often a film-forming sealer that controls dust and adds a soft sheen. Do not seal the inside of the firebox, which reaches temperatures most sealers are not designed for. Make sure the fireplace has been cold for at least a day before applying anything.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should historic or soft brick be treated?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Old, soft brick and lime mortar need to breathe more than modern brick. If sealing is necessary, use only a highly breathable penetrating repellent and test it first. Many preservation specialists recommend against sealing historic masonry unless there is a specific water problem that repairs alone cannot solve. A non-breathable film on historic brick can be very damaging.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is painting brick a good alternative?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but paint is a long-term commitment and changes how the wall manages moisture. If you paint, use a breathable masonry paint or mineral silicate paint rather than ordinary exterior latex, and make sure the brick is dry and free of efflorescence. A clear penetrating repellent is less invasive if your goal is only water protection.
